\'95\
Fragmented observability makes it hard to connect\
scaling events to business impact, leaving teams blind\
to user-facing consequences.\
Without real-time visibility into scaling behavior, teams\
are prone to misconfiguring resource requests \'97 leading\
to wasted capacity or performance bottlenecks.\
Ignoring pod evictions and resource contention,\
resulting in unexpected application failures.\
Autoscaling tuning is an art form. HPA utilizes multiple\
occurrences of an observed metric to balance temporary\
deviations and avoid \'93flapping\'94 (frequent scaling in/out).\
HPA and VPA can conflict in dynamic environments.\
HPA scales pod count based on resource utilization, while\
VPA adjusts pod resource requests, potentially causing\
restarts. Coordination is needed to prevent conflicts.\
JVM-based workloads add tuning complexity (e.g., cold\
starts, garbage collection, memory management), often\
interfering with autoscaling and potentially contributing\
to performance bottlenecks.\
Kubernetes issues can cause widespread disruption,\
as the platform now powers many of the most critical\
business services.\
