\fs88 \cf2 Observability insights and best practices\

\fs36 Not thinking about scaling in advance leads teams to reactively\
adjust limits, modify thresholds, and deploy resources without fully\
grasping the consequences. These guesswork-driven changes can\
either lead to unnecessary costs, ongoing performance bottlenecks,\
or even worse, eroded brand reputation.\
To maintain optimal Kubernetes performance, teams need\
observability-driven insights that provide clarity into autoscaling\
behavior and resource utilization:\
\'95\
Unify your observability data to capture the full picture of\
Kubernetes performance, ensuring that scaling and tuning\
decisions are informed by end-to-end insights \'97 from\
infrastructure to customer impact.\
\'95\
Correlate autoscaling events with external service dependencies\
to identify latency bottlenecks caused by uneven scaling across\
microservices or databases.\
\'95\
Monitor real-time scaling behavior to detect and resolve delays\
before they impact users.\
\'95\
Track key resource and performance metrics (like CPU, memory,\
and RED signals) to spot bottlenecks early and prevent degraded\
user experiences.\
\'95\
Leverage baselines, forecasts, and business demand to fine tune\
autoscaling configurations, optimizing efficiency over time.\
\'95\
Track node-level resource saturation to detect pod evictions early\
and ensure workloads are scheduled efficiently across the cluster.\
\'95\
Analyze pod startup times and correlate with autoscaling events\
to fine-tune scaling triggers, especially for latency-sensitive\
applications.\
\'95\
Ensure balanced resource allocation by leveraging observability\
to avoid over-provisioning (wasting compute) or under-provisioning\
(causing service degradation) capacity.\
\'95\
Stay current on evolving autoscaling strategies beyond pods, and\
use observability to track how node, cluster, and workload scaling\
impacts overall performance.\
Observability acts as a performance tuning tool, much like a vehicle\'92s\
onboard diagnostics system that continuously tracks performance\
over time. Just as modern cars use telemetry data to optimize\
engine efficiency and anticipate issues, Kubernetes teams can\
analyze historical metrics to fine-tune autoscaling strategies. This\
approach ensures Kubernetes scaling decisions are as precise as a\
finely tuned engine \'97 delivering the right resources at the right time\
with no unnecessary delays.\
